  {
    "id": "lesson_question_formation",
    "level": "A1",
    "title": "Question Formation (Fragesätze)",
    "subtitle": "How to ask Yes/No questions and W-Questions in German",
    "category": "Satzbau",
    "summary": "To ask a Yes/No question, swap the Subject and Verb (VSO). To ask a W-Question, start with a question word (Was, Woher, Wie) followed immediately by the verb.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "questions_explanation",
        "type": "explanation",
        "title": "Zwei Arten von Fragen (Two Types of Questions)",
        "explanation_payload": {
          "text": "In German, questions are built in two ways depending on whether you want a Yes/No answer or specific information:",
          "bullet_points": [
            "1. Ja/Nein-Fragen (Yes/No Questions): Swap Subject and Verb! The verb jumps to Position 1 (VSO).",
            "2. W-Fragen (Information Questions): Start with a W-Word (Was, Woher, Wie, Wo), then Verb at Position 2, then Subject."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "questions_formula_yn",
        "type": "formula",
        "title": "Ja/Nein-Fragen (VSO Structure)",
        "formula_payload": {
          "formula_structure": "Verb (Pos 1) + Subject (Pos 2) + Object (Pos 3)?",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "position": "Position 1",
              "label": "Verb (Kicker)",
              "example_word": "Sprichst",
              "color_tag": "primary"
            },
            {
              "position": "Position 2",
              "label": "Subjekt",
              "example_word": "du",
              "color_tag": "neutral"
            },
            {
              "position": "Position 3",
              "label": "Objekt",
              "example_word": "Deutsch?",
              "color_tag": "accent"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "questions_formula_w",
        "type": "formula",
        "title": "W-Fragen Structure",
        "formula_payload": {
          "formula_structure": "Fragewort + Verb (Pos 2) + Subject + ...?",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "position": "W-Wort",
              "label": "Fragewort",
              "example_word": "Woher",
              "color_tag": "warning"
            },
            {
              "position": "Position 2",
              "label": "Verb",
              "example_word": "kommst",
              "color_tag": "primary"
            },
            {
              "position": "Position 3",
              "label": "Subjekt",
              "example_word": "du?",
              "color_tag": "neutral"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "questions_examples",
        "type": "examples",
        "title": "Frage & Antwort Beispiele",
        "example_payload": [
          {
            "german": "Sprichst du Deutsch? – Ja, ein bisschen.",
            "english": "Do you speak German? – Yes, a little bit.",
            "note": "Verb 'Sprichst' at Position 1.",
            "highlighted_words": [
              "Sprichst",
              "du"
            ]
          },
          {
            "german": "Woher kommst du? – Ich komme aus Spanien.",
            "english": "Where are you from? – I come from Spain.",
            "note": "Question word 'Woher' + Verb 'kommst' at Pos 2.",
            "highlighted_words": [
              "Woher",
              "kommst"
            ]
          },
          {
            "german": "Hat sie Hunger? – Ja, sie hat Hunger.",
            "english": "Is she hungry? – Yes, she is hungry.",
            "note": "Flipped from 'Sie hat Hunger' → 'Hat sie Hunger?'",
            "highlighted_words": [
              "Hat",
              "sie"
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "lesson_noun_genders_articles",
    "level": "A1",
    "title": "Noun Genders & Definite Articles",
    "subtitle": "Mastering Der, Die, Das",
    "category": "Nomen & Artikel",
    "summary": "Every German noun is assigned one of three genders: Masculine (der), Feminine (die), or Neuter (das). Always learn the article together with the noun!",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "articles_explanation",
        "type": "explanation",
        "title": "Die drei Geschlechter (The Three Genders)",
        "explanation_payload": {
          "text": "Unlike English, which uses 'the' for everything, German nouns have grammatical gender. The gender doesn't always reflect biological gender—it is purely grammatical!",
          "bullet_points": [
            "der = Masculine (e.g. der Tisch / the table)",
            "die = Feminine (e.g. die Katze / the cat)",
            "das = Neuter (e.g. das Haus / the house)",
            "Golden Rule: Never memorize 'Tisch' alone. Always memorize 'der Tisch'!"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "articles_table",
        "type": "table",
        "title": "Bestimmte Artikel Übersicht",
        "table_payload": {
          "headers": [
            "Genus (Gender)",
            "Artikel",
            "Beispiel Wort",
            "Bedeutung"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Maskulin",
              "der",
              "der Tisch",
              "the table"
            ],
            [
              "Feminin",
              "die",
              "die Katze",
              "the cat"
            ],
            [
              "Neutral",
              "das",
              "das Haus",
              "the house"
            ],
            [
              "Plural (All genders)",
              "die",
              "die Bücher",
              "the books"
            ]
          ],
          "footnote": "Note: ALL plural nouns in German use 'die' in the Nominative case!"
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "articles_tip",
        "type": "tip",
        "title": "Lehrer-Tipp für Vokabeln",
        "tip_payload": {
          "title": "Lerne Nomen immer mit Farbe!",
          "content": "Use color-coding in your app/flashcards: Blue for 'der' (Masculine), Red for 'die' (Feminine), and Green for 'das' (Neuter). Your brain connects colors to genders much faster!",
          "is_warning": false
        }
      }
    ]
  },
